call_details.response:
The board state has pieces of both players numbering 15 each, with X to move. The only non-full columns (legal moves) are 0, 5 and 6; every other column has its top cell occupied.

All 69 possible windows of length 4 were enumerated. After discarding those already permanently blocked by a mixture of X and O stones, the only remaining live winning lines are:

- the single diagonal window that is completed by an X stone at cell (3,5);
- the horizontal window on row 1 that is completed by X stones at both (1,5) and (1,6);
- the two vertical windows inside column 0;
- the two vertical windows inside column 5;
- the horizontal window on row 2 completed by an O stone at (2,5);
- the horizontal window on row 0 completed by O stones at both (0,5) and (0,6).

No other line can ever become four-in-a-row.

Because the live lines intersect only inside columns 0/5/6, every threat that can ever appear is a single-column threat. Consequently neither player possesses a move that simultaneously creates two distinct threats (a fork). The defender can therefore always answer the unique threatened column on the subsequent turn.  

Under optimal replies the concrete forcing sequences that arise are:

- X opening column 5 produces the forced continuation X-O-X inside that column; O can then always answer the residual threats that appear on columns 5 and 6.
- Any attempt by X to accumulate four consecutive stones inside column 0 requires three “passes” (moves by O outside column 0). At most two such passes can be forced (the two obligatory answers inside column 5). Hence X never obtains a vertical threat of length 3 that O cannot immediately block.
- Symmetric reasoning shows that O likewise can never obtain four consecutive stones in column 0 or column 5, nor both cells of the row-0 horizontal.

All three legal opening moves therefore lead to a completely filled board with no four-in-a-row, i.e., a draw. Column 5 is selected because it is the unique column that intersects every live winning line; occupying it immediately seizes control of the most constrained sector of the board and forces O into the longest defensive chain.

Final Answer: 5

